2025-07-17 21:13:53 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 6: 数据 = {'id': '37f1b207-94ea-41c8-b0c4-c4f36303470e', 'document_id': '8f91fd80-92de-4ed1-ba13-da386278bb88', 'content': 'GmbH, Oberkochen, Germany) was also used to examine the morphologies of the GP/NR and FA/ NR samples. The samples were fast cut with a razor blade to create smooth sample surfaces before the optical microscopy. ## 3 RESULTS AND DISCUSSION ### 3.1 | Characteristics of geopolymer and fly ash particles Figure 4 shows the FTIR spectra of geopolymer and fly ash, and the peak assignments are summarized in Table 5. It can be seen that the FTIR spectrum of FA showed intense absorption at $1,{100}{\\mathrm{\\;{cm}}}^{-1}$ , which corresponds to the Si-O-Si stretching vibrations. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {23}\\right\\rbrack }$ In addition, the peak at ${681}{\\mathrm{\\;{cm}}}^{-1}$ shows the vibrations of ${\\mathrm{{Al}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ and that at $1,{007}{\\mathrm{\\;{cm}}}^{-1}$ represents Al-O-Al stretching vibrations. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {24}\\right\\rbrack }$ Furthermore, the peak at $3,{445}{\\mathrm{\\;{cm}}}^{-1}$ indicates $\\mathrm{O} - \\mathrm{H}$ groups in the FA particles. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {25}\\right\\rbrack }$ In Figure 4, it is also clear that the FTIR spectra of geopolymer and fly ash were very similar. In them, the absorption peak at $1,{001}{\\mathrm{\\;{cm}}}^{-1}$ is assigned to the asymmetric stretching vibrations of Si-O-T bonds (T-tetrahedral Al or Si). It is noted that the peak at $1,{001}{\\mathrm{\\;{cm}}}^{-1}$ refers to the geopolymerization of aluminosilicate source material in FA. Furthermore, a shift in the main band (i.e., at the wave number of $1,{086}{\\mathrm{\\;{cm}}}^{-1}$ ) shows some changes in the angle and the length of Si-O-Si bonds in the gel. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {26}\\right\\rbrack }$ This indicates the extent of geopolymerization reactions or of the formation of geopolymers, and the reaction kinetics. That is, the movement of the main band allows monitoring the geopolymerization kinetics. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {26}\\right\\rbrack }$ In Figure 4, two small bands at the wavenumbers 1,625 and $3,{452}{\\mathrm{\\;{cm}}}^{-1}$ in the spectrum of the geopolymer correspond to the stretching vibrations of silanol groups and the hydrogen bonds of water with adjacent (vicinal) silanols (-OH).', 'chunk_index': 6, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
