2025-07-17 21:13:53 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 16: 数据 = {'id': '5a2a5f38-9dfd-4add-acac-30ad4448716e', 'document_id': '8f91fd80-92de-4ed1-ba13-da386278bb88', 'content': '<table><tr><td>Sample</td><td>Min. torque $\\left( {{M}_{\\mathrm{L}},\\mathrm{{dNm}}}\\right)$</td><td>Max. torque $\\left( {{M}_{\\mathrm{H}},\\mathrm{d}\\mathrm{{Nm}}}\\right)$</td><td>Delta torque $\\left( {{\\mathbf{M}}_{\\mathbf{H}}\\mathbf{ - }{\\mathbf{M}}_{\\mathbf{L}}\\mathbf{, d}\\mathbf{N}\\mathbf{m}}\\right)$</td><td>Scorch time (ts2, min)</td><td>Cure time (tc90, min)</td><td>Cure rate index $\\left( {\\mathbf{{CRI}},{\\mathbf{{min}}}^{-1}}\\right)$</td></tr><tr><td>Gum NR</td><td>1.55</td><td>8.64</td><td>7.09</td><td>2.17</td><td>7.12</td><td>20.2</td></tr><tr><td>NR-FA10</td><td>1.24</td><td>8.29</td><td>7.05</td><td>1.19</td><td>4.33</td><td>31.8</td></tr><tr><td>NR-GP10</td><td>2.06</td><td>9.57</td><td>7.51</td><td>0.54</td><td>4.49</td><td>25.3</td></tr><tr><td>NR-GP20</td><td>2.01</td><td>8.90</td><td>6.89</td><td>1.03</td><td>4.08</td><td>32.8</td></tr><tr><td>NR-GP30</td><td>2.05</td><td>8.52</td><td>6.45</td><td>0.58</td><td>2.59</td><td>49.8</td></tr><tr><td>NR-GP40</td><td>2.01</td><td>8.60</td><td>6.59</td><td>0.58</td><td>2.57</td><td>50.3</td></tr></table> Figure 8 shows schematically the proposed GP and FA structures and their distribution in the NR matrix. The GP had more homogeneous dispersion with smaller agglomerates and less voids in the NR matrix (Figure 8a), and its larger specific surface caused more filler-rubber interactions increasing the curing torque (Figure 5) and improving the mechanical properties (Figure 9). Conversely, the FA gave larger agglomerates and more voids in the NR matrix (Figure 8b). The FA had higher contents of ${\\mathrm{{Fe}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ and ${\\mathrm{{Al}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ (Table 4) and larger particles (Figure 3), which might explain its tendency to agglomerate.\nTABLE 6 Cure characteristic of gum and FA- and GP-filled NR vulcanizates with various geopolymer loadings and 10 phr of fly ash\n![01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_7_128_1449_1010_753_0.jpg](images/01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_7_128_1449_1010_753_0.jpg)\nFIGURE 8 Schematic representations of geopolymer (GP) and fly ash (FA) structures and their dispersion in the natural rubber (NR) matrix, for the NR/GP (a) and NR/FA (b) composites\n![01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_8_626_133_1014_781_0.jpg](images/01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_8_626_133_1014_781_0.jpg)\nFIGURE 9 Stress-strain curves of gum, fly ash- and geopolymer (GP)-filled natural rubber vulcanizates with various geopolymer loadings or 10 phr fly ash. (The insert provides magnified view of below 100% extensions.)\n### 3.5 | Mechanical properties', 'chunk_index': 16, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
