2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 0: 数据 = {'id': '0b2df9e9-98e0-413d-8019-d0cd97509113', 'document_id': '98484ebb-25d1-4d80-8f85-407514723f39', 'content': '# Novel natural rubber composites with geopolymer filler\nHassarutai Yangthong | Skulrat Pichaiyut | Saysunee Jumrat | Suwaluk Wisunthorn Charoen Nakason\nFaculty of Science and Industrial Technology, Prince of Songkla University, Surat Thani, Thailand\n## Correspondence\nSuwaluk Wisunthorn and Charoen Nakason, Faculty of Science and Industrial Technology, Prince of Songkla University, Surat Thani, Thailand.\nEmails: suwaluk.w@psu.ac.th (SW); charoen.na@psu.ac.th (CN)\nFunding information\nPSU NR-IRI (Natural Rubber Innovation Research Institute, Grant/Award Number: SIT590315S\n## Abstract\nThe main objective of this study was to apply geopolymer (GP) particles as novelty fillers in natural rubber (NR) composites with various loadings $(0,{10},{20},{30}$ , and 40 phr). Influence of GP particles on cure characteristics, mechanical, dynamic, thermal, and morphological properties of the NR/geopolymer composites was investigated. It was found that incorporation of geopolymer particles significantly affected the cure characteristics and the mechanical properties. That is, faster curing was observed due to acceleration by metal oxide impurities. It was also found that ${100}\\%$ modulus and tensile strength increased with the addition of ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}\\mathrm{{GP}}$ . This is attributed to the physical interactions of GP particles with NR molecules. In addition, the Payne effect and improved thermal stability were also observed in the GP-filled NR composites. It was concluded that ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}$ of GP in NR composites might be suitable for some industrial applications.\n## KEYWORDS\ncomposites, geopolymer, mechanical properties, natural rubber, thermal properties\n## 1 INTRODUCTION', 'chunk_index': 0, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': {'paragraph_index': 0, 'original_length': 56}}}
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:195 - 分块 0 创建成功: 0b2df9e9-98e0-413d-8019-d0cd97509113
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:181 - 创建分块 1: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 1: 数据 = {'id': '3773c2aa-1e02-4197-ae23-108943c3d81d', 'document_id': '98484ebb-25d1-4d80-8f85-407514723f39', 'content': 'with NR molecules. In addition, the Payne effect and improved thermal stability were also observed in the GP-filled NR composites. It was concluded that ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}$ of GP in NR composites might be suitable for some industrial applications. ## KEYWORDS composites, geopolymer, mechanical properties, natural rubber, thermal properties ## 1 INTRODUCTION Polymer composites combine a polymer matrix with fillers. They are attractive in industrial applications and for academic research, due to control of the material properties. Natural rubber (NR) as a green and renewable polymer is widely used as a raw polymer material industrially, including in automotive parts and tires, because of its excellent mechanical and elastic properties. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack 1\\right\\rbrack }$ However, fillers are necessary in most cases to modify the NR properties for specific applications. A wide variety of particulate fillers are used in the rubber industry for multiple purposes, including reinforcement, reduced material costs, and improved processing. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack 2\\right\\rbrack }$ The most important fillers in rubber compounding are the conventional synthetic fillers based on carbon black and silica. They reinforce and enhance various properties such as tear strength, tensile strength, hardness, and abrasion resistance. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack 3\\right\\rbrack }$ The incorporation of solid particles with large specific surface, such as synthetic silica, quartz, carbonaceous materials, and metal oxides, provided ample filler surface to interact with the rubber molecules, and hence these are very effective in improving rubber properties. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack 4\\right\\rbrack }$ Synthetic silicas, for instance precipitated silica, possess siloxane and acidic silanol groups (Si-OH), ${}^{\\left\\lbrack 5\\right\\rbrack }$ and have also been widely used in the polymer industries. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack 6\\right\\rbrack }$ Typically the silica surfaces are hydrophilic, so they have strong tendency to adsorb moisture. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack 7\\right\\rbrack }$ Furthermore, when large amounts of silica are added, the compound viscosity is increased substantially due to strong silica-silica interactions, ${}^{\\left\\lbrack 8\\right\\rbrack }$ and the production of these conventional fillers is highly energy-consuming. Thus, alternative fillers from renewable resources are of considerable interest to improve the properties of rubber while improving sustainability and green aspects of the rubber life cycle.', 'chunk_index': 1, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:195 - 分块 1 创建成功: 3773c2aa-1e02-4197-ae23-108943c3d81d
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:181 - 创建分块 2: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 2: 数据 = {'id': 'b38c5eba-c0e3-4311-8060-79dfb60654fd', 'document_id': '98484ebb-25d1-4d80-8f85-407514723f39', 'content': "compound viscosity is increased substantially due to strong silica-silica interactions, ${}^{\\left\\lbrack 8\\right\\rbrack }$ and the production of these conventional fillers is highly energy-consuming. Thus, alternative fillers from renewable resources are of considerable interest to improve the properties of rubber while improving sustainability and green aspects of the rubber life cycle. In recent years, silica from natural sources has attracted attention as an alternative reinforcing filler in natural and synthetic rubber, because of low cost, good mechanical properties, dimensional stability, and environmental issues. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack 9\\right\\rbrack }$ Types of natural fillers exploited in rubber composites include fly ash (FA), ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {10}\\right\\rbrack }$ rice husk ash (RHA), ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {11}\\right\\rbrack }$ oil palm ash (OPA), ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {12}\\right\\rbrack }$ bagasse fiber ash (BFA), ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {13}\\right\\rbrack }$ cellulose fiber, ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {14}\\right\\rbrack }$ coconut shell powder (CSP), ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {15}\\right\\rbrack }$ and lignin. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {16}\\right\\rbrack }$ The FA is a byproduct from the combustion of pulverized coal in electric power generating plants. It is a potential alternative filler in rubber materials with nearly ${40} - {50}\\mathrm{{wt}}\\%$ silica content. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {10}\\right\\rbrack }$ In addition, FA contains various chemical elements, including aluminum, iron, calcium, magnesium, sulfur, sodium, potassium, and carbon. On the other hand, RHA filler has also been tested in natural rubber compounds. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {17}\\right\\rbrack }$ It was found that the incorporation of RHA significantly affected cure characteristics and mechanical properties of NR composites. That is, RHA caused faster curing and increased Young's modulus, tensile strength and other related strength properties. In addition, OPA filler was also used in rubber compounds, and tensile strength and elongation at break improved by about ${16}\\%$ and ${7.4}\\%$ , respectively. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {18}\\right\\rbrack }$", 'chunk_index': 2, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:195 - 分块 2 创建成功: b38c5eba-c0e3-4311-8060-79dfb60654fd
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:181 - 创建分块 3: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 3: 数据 = {'id': '9b32cc3f-cd2b-470d-9efc-ff9315306456', 'document_id': '98484ebb-25d1-4d80-8f85-407514723f39', 'content': "mechanical properties of NR composites. That is, RHA caused faster curing and increased Young's modulus, tensile strength and other related strength properties. In addition, OPA filler was also used in rubber compounds, and tensile strength and elongation at break improved by about ${16}\\%$ and ${7.4}\\%$ , respectively. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {18}\\right\\rbrack }$ ![01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_1_126_130_1011_755_0.jpg](images/01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_1_126_130_1011_755_0.jpg)\nFIGURE 1 Geopolymerization reactions of fly ash with alkaline solution. $\\mathrm{M} +$ refers to alkali cations\nGeopolymers (GPs) are polymerized chains or networks of inorganic aluminosilicate molecules linked with covalent bonds. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {19}\\right\\rbrack }$ They are synthesized at room temperature from predominantly silica and aluminum materials of geological origin, or with by-products such as fly ash, as geopolymer-ization process shown in Figure 1. The geopolymers are a comparatively new type of material containing pozzolan components and alkaline solution. It is noted that fly ash has been frequently used as the pozzolan material in the synthesis of GPs. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {20}\\right\\rbrack }$ Therefore, geopolymers can be produced from waste materials and thus reduce environmental problems. In particular, a new and green solution is to utilize fly ash to avoid its negative impacts on environment and ecology, instead trapping the toxic trace metal elements in geopolymer structures. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {21}\\right\\rbrack }$ The fly ash-based geopolymers typically exhibit superior mechanical properties with low density and cage-like crystalline structure. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {19}\\right\\rbrack }$ Furthermore, their durability has been recognized as nearly comparable to that of hydrated Portland cement, and they can be used as green cement. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {22}\\right\\rbrack }$ Geopolymers might also have use as new alternative fillers that improve various properties of rubber composites. This is because geopolymers contain Si and Al ions that could form linkages with organic polymer molecules. Therefore, the geopolymer might interact with the rubber molecules, which enhances reinforcement effects. These might be innovative and advantageous fillers in natural rubber composites. Therefore, the novel NR/GP composites which were prepared in this work had benefit over the conventional fillers in particular low energy-consuming process.", 'chunk_index': 3, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:195 - 分块 3 创建成功: 9b32cc3f-cd2b-470d-9efc-ff9315306456
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:181 - 创建分块 4: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 4: 数据 = {'id': '4dfb0ac2-d58f-40ef-8e08-1aaa14de4624', 'document_id': '98484ebb-25d1-4d80-8f85-407514723f39', 'content': 'with organic polymer molecules. Therefore, the geopolymer might interact with the rubber molecules, which enhances reinforcement effects. These might be innovative and advantageous fillers in natural rubber composites. Therefore, the novel NR/GP composites which were prepared in this work had benefit over the conventional fillers in particular low energy-consuming process. Cure characteristics of the GP/NR and FA/NR composites were determined using a moving die rheometer (MDR) (Monsanto Co., Ltd., OH, USA). The measurements were performed at the fixed ${1.66}\\mathrm{\\;{Hz}}$ oscillation frequency with 1 arc degree amplitude, at ${160}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . The scorch time (ts2), optimum cure time (tc90), and minimum $\\left( {M}_{H}\\right)$ and maximum $\\left( {M}_{L}\\right)$ torque were recorded. Then, the compounds were vulcanized in an electrically heated plate press, model PR1D-W400L450PM (Charon Tut Co., LTD., Bangkok, Thailand) under 70 bar compression pressure at ${160}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . The GP/NR and FA/NR vulcanizate sheets were then cooled down to room temperature and conditioned for ${24}\\mathrm{{hr}}$ . Dumbbell-shaped test specimens were eventually prepared by die cutting for further testing and characterization.\n![01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_3_131_134_728_589_0.jpg](images/01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_3_131_134_728_589_0.jpg)\nFIGURE 2 Particle size distributions of powder form geopolymer and fly ash used as fillers\n### 2.6 | Mechanical properties\nThe tensile properties of the GP/NR and FA/NR composites were determined using a universal tensile testing machine (Tinius Olsen, model 10ST, Salfords, England) with a crosshead speed of ${200}\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ according to ISO 527. The dumbbell shape used was ISO 527 (type 5A). The stress-strain curves were recorded, and tensile strength and elongation at break were estimated from these curves.\n### 2.7 | Dynamic properties', 'chunk_index': 4, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:195 - 分块 4 创建成功: 4dfb0ac2-d58f-40ef-8e08-1aaa14de4624
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:181 - 创建分块 5: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-17 21:13:54 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 5: 数据 = {'id': '6a7600c6-d900-4bf8-91b8-3459d2675f15', 'document_id': '98484ebb-25d1-4d80-8f85-407514723f39', 'content': 'tensile testing machine (Tinius Olsen, model 10ST, Salfords, England) with a crosshead speed of ${200}\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ according to ISO 527. The dumbbell shape used was ISO 527 (type 5A). The stress-strain curves were recorded, and tensile strength and elongation at break were estimated from these curves. ### 2.7 | Dynamic properties Dynamic properties in terms of storage modulus $\\left( {\\mathrm{G}}^{\\prime }\\right)$ were investigated using RPA 2000 (Alpha Technologies, Akron, USA). The strain sweep from 0.56% to 500% was performed at ${80}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ at the constant $1\\mathrm{\\;{Hz}}$ frequency.\n### 2.8 | Thermal properties\nThermal stability of the GP/NR and FA/NR samples was characterized by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA; STA 6000, Perkin Elmer Inc., Norwalk, CT, USA). A sample of approximately ${20}\\mathrm{{mg}}$ was placed in a ceramic pan and then heated at ${10}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ rate across the temperature range from 30 to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , under nitrogen atmosphere.\nTABLE 3 Physical properties of fly ash and geopolymer particles\n<table><tr><td>Properties</td><td>Fly ash</td><td>Geopolymer</td></tr><tr><td>Mean particle size $\\left( {\\mu \\mathrm{m}}\\right)$</td><td>13.5</td><td>9.5</td></tr><tr><td>Density $\\left( {\\mathrm{g}/{\\mathrm{{cm}}}^{3}}\\right)$</td><td>1.04</td><td>1.05</td></tr><tr><td>pH</td><td>12.4</td><td>12.0</td></tr></table>\n### 2.9 | Morphological characterization\nThe morphological properties of GP/NR and FA/NR samples were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (FEI-Quanta 400, Jeol Ltd., Tokyo, Japan). The specimens were first cryogenically fractured to create fresh cross-sectional surfaces that were gold coated by sputtering under vacuum before imaging by SEM. A metallographic optical microscope (Carl Zeiss Microscopy GmbH, Oberkochen, Germany) was also used to examine the morphologies of the GP/NR and FA/ NR samples. The samples were fast cut with a razor blade to create smooth sample surfaces before the optical microscopy.\n## 3 RESULTS AND DISCUSSION\n### 3.1 | Characteristics of geopolymer and fly ash particles', 'chunk_index': 5, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
