2025-07-17 21:21:50 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 11: 数据 = {'id': '31164bcc-dcdc-485e-ae58-8a859e25435f', 'document_id': 'c04f58cb-a224-4dbd-952d-01fb3c7d6edf', 'content': 'particles are larger than ${30\\mu }\\mathrm{m}$ . TABLE 4 Chemical compositions of fly ash and geopolymer particles ![01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_4_903_134_731_635_0.jpg](images/01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_4_903_134_731_635_0.jpg) FIGURE 4 Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy spectra of geopolymer and fly ash particles [Colour figure can be viewed at wileyonlinelibrary.com] ### 3.2 | Geopolymerization and chemical composition of geopolymer and fly ash particles Figure 1 shows the geopolymerization process based on the reaction of fly ash and alkaline solution. It can be seen that the alkali facilitated decomposition of aluminosilicate in the fly ash and then favored polycondensation. The reaction proceeds under mild temperatures in the range from 25 to ${90}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , so it is considered energy efficient and practical. Apparently, the reactions between fly ash and alkali and condensation of the resultant ${\\mathrm{{Si}}}^{4 + }$ and ${\\mathrm{{Al}}}^{3 + }$ species took place, followed by nucleation, oligomerization, and polymerization. This finally led to new aluminosilicate-based polymers with amorphous three-dimensional geopolymer networks. The fly ash-based geopolymer paste was cast into a mold at room temperature to settle in its final form. The alkali activation of fly ash is critical to geopolymerization. It is known that the alkaline solutions (i.e., ${\\mathrm{{Na}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{{NaOH}}$ ), silica, alumina, and aluminosilicate in fly ash, are important factors controlling geopolymerization and structure of the final geopolymer. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {27}\\right\\rbrack }$ Upon hydrolysis, the -Si-O-Si- or -Si-O-Al- bonds in aluminosilicate were broken, and the active ${\\mathrm{{Al}}}^{3 + }$ and ${\\mathrm{{Si}}}^{4 + }$ species were released. These active species shared electrons with the $4{\\mathrm{O}}^{2 - }$ ions to form nuclei and aluminosilicate oligomers of tetrahedral ${\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{4}$ and ${\\mathrm{{AlO}}}_{4}$ . Then, the aluminosilicate oligomers could be linked to form polysialate with -Al-O-Si- chains, polysialate siloxo -Al-O-Si-Si- chains, and polysialate disiloxo -Al-O-Si-Si-Si-chains. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {28}\\right\\rbrack }$ The geopolymer formed here consisted of ${30}\\mathrm{{wt}}\\%$ ${\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2},{17.9}\\mathrm{{wt}}\\% \\mathrm{{CaO}},{12.', 'chunk_index': 11, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
