2025-07-17 21:21:50 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:181 - 创建分块 20: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-17 21:21:50 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 20: 数据 = {'id': '1f363e5c-dc55-4e4d-a200-b9aab8f69bfc', 'document_id': 'c04f58cb-a224-4dbd-952d-01fb3c7d6edf', 'content': 'the decomposition temperatures at $5\\% ,{10}\\% ,{50}\\%$ , and ${80}\\%$ weight losses (Table 7) for the GP- or FA-filled NR composites were higher than corresponding temperatures for the gum NR vulcanizates. This might be due to the dispersed geopolymer and fly ash fillers enhancing the thermal stability of NR composites. Geopolymer (GP) was successfully used as an alternative novelty filler in NR composite vulcanizates. It was found that the GP-filled NR composites had faster scorch and cure times, and higher cure rate index than the gum NR vulcanizate. Furthermore, the NR composites with 10 phr GP exhibited higher ${100}\\%$ modulus and tensile strength than the NR/FA composite with similar level of fly ash (FA) loading, due to the stronger interactions of GP and NR matrix and the better GP filler distribution with smaller aggregates. Furthermore, larger cavities or voids were observed in the composites with high GP contents (i.e., 30 and 40 phr), as excessive filler loading caused large agglomerates and cavities in the composites because the adhesion of NR and GP was not sufficiently strong. In addition, the NR composite with 10 phr GP exhibited finer and better dispersion of the particles in the NR matrix than seen in the NR/FA composite. It was also found that in the low-strain regime, the storage modulus ${\\mathrm{G}}^{\\prime }$ of NR composites increased with GP filler loading, while the FA-filled NR composite had the lowest value. This relates to the Payne effect and filler-filler interactions in the composites. In addition, the thermal stability of GP-filled NR composites was slightly improved from that of the gum NR. Therefore, it is concluded that that ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}$ of GP in NR composites might be suitable for some industrial applications.\n## ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\nThe authors would like to acknowledge financial support by the PSU NR-IRI (Natural Rubber Innovation Research Institute, contract no. SIT590315S. Also, Prince of Songkla University, Surat Thani Campus, Thailand, is gratefully acknowledged for providing access to its facilities.\n## ORCID\nCharoen Nakason http://orcid.org/0000-0003-1631-9369\n## REFERENCES\n[1] A. D. Roberts, Natural Rubber Science and Technology, Oxford University Press, New York 1988.\n[2] Y. Zhang, Q. Liu, Q. Zhang, Y. Lu, Appl. Clay Sci. 2010, 50, 255.\n[3] E. H. Andrews, Rubber Chem. Technol. 1963, 36, 325.', 'chunk_index': 20, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
