2025-07-17 21:21:50 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 4: 数据 = {'id': 'e5a2ce22-4b34-46d5-b455-a99fff3cbb34', 'document_id': '00dee3de-5908-4d9d-a0e2-dbbce470ae86', 'content': 'with organic polymer molecules. Therefore, the geopolymer might interact with the rubber molecules, which enhances reinforcement effects. These might be innovative and advantageous fillers in natural rubber composites. Therefore, the novel NR/GP composites which were prepared in this work had benefit over the conventional fillers in particular low energy-consuming process. Cure characteristics of the GP/NR and FA/NR composites were determined using a moving die rheometer (MDR) (Monsanto Co., Ltd., OH, USA). The measurements were performed at the fixed ${1.66}\\mathrm{\\;{Hz}}$ oscillation frequency with 1 arc degree amplitude, at ${160}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . The scorch time (ts2), optimum cure time (tc90), and minimum $\\left( {M}_{H}\\right)$ and maximum $\\left( {M}_{L}\\right)$ torque were recorded. Then, the compounds were vulcanized in an electrically heated plate press, model PR1D-W400L450PM (Charon Tut Co., LTD., Bangkok, Thailand) under 70 bar compression pressure at ${160}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . The GP/NR and FA/NR vulcanizate sheets were then cooled down to room temperature and conditioned for ${24}\\mathrm{{hr}}$ . Dumbbell-shaped test specimens were eventually prepared by die cutting for further testing and characterization.\n![01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_3_131_134_728_589_0.jpg](images/01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_3_131_134_728_589_0.jpg)\nFIGURE 2 Particle size distributions of powder form geopolymer and fly ash used as fillers\n### 2.6 | Mechanical properties\nThe tensile properties of the GP/NR and FA/NR composites were determined using a universal tensile testing machine (Tinius Olsen, model 10ST, Salfords, England) with a crosshead speed of ${200}\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ according to ISO 527. The dumbbell shape used was ISO 527 (type 5A). The stress-strain curves were recorded, and tensile strength and elongation at break were estimated from these curves.\n### 2.7 | Dynamic properties', 'chunk_index': 4, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
