2025-07-17 21:21:50 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 5: 数据 = {'id': 'fd6bec87-8717-4440-9836-73944f4948fb', 'document_id': '00dee3de-5908-4d9d-a0e2-dbbce470ae86', 'content': 'tensile testing machine (Tinius Olsen, model 10ST, Salfords, England) with a crosshead speed of ${200}\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ according to ISO 527. The dumbbell shape used was ISO 527 (type 5A). The stress-strain curves were recorded, and tensile strength and elongation at break were estimated from these curves. ### 2.7 | Dynamic properties Dynamic properties in terms of storage modulus $\\left( {\\mathrm{G}}^{\\prime }\\right)$ were investigated using RPA 2000 (Alpha Technologies, Akron, USA). The strain sweep from 0.56% to 500% was performed at ${80}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ at the constant $1\\mathrm{\\;{Hz}}$ frequency.\n### 2.8 | Thermal properties\nThermal stability of the GP/NR and FA/NR samples was characterized by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA; STA 6000, Perkin Elmer Inc., Norwalk, CT, USA). A sample of approximately ${20}\\mathrm{{mg}}$ was placed in a ceramic pan and then heated at ${10}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ rate across the temperature range from 30 to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , under nitrogen atmosphere.\nTABLE 3 Physical properties of fly ash and geopolymer particles\n<table><tr><td>Properties</td><td>Fly ash</td><td>Geopolymer</td></tr><tr><td>Mean particle size $\\left( {\\mu \\mathrm{m}}\\right)$</td><td>13.5</td><td>9.5</td></tr><tr><td>Density $\\left( {\\mathrm{g}/{\\mathrm{{cm}}}^{3}}\\right)$</td><td>1.04</td><td>1.05</td></tr><tr><td>pH</td><td>12.4</td><td>12.0</td></tr></table>\n### 2.9 | Morphological characterization\nThe morphological properties of GP/NR and FA/NR samples were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (FEI-Quanta 400, Jeol Ltd., Tokyo, Japan). The specimens were first cryogenically fractured to create fresh cross-sectional surfaces that were gold coated by sputtering under vacuum before imaging by SEM. A metallographic optical microscope (Carl Zeiss Microscopy GmbH, Oberkochen, Germany) was also used to examine the morphologies of the GP/NR and FA/ NR samples. The samples were fast cut with a razor blade to create smooth sample surfaces before the optical microscopy.\n## 3 RESULTS AND DISCUSSION\n### 3.1 | Characteristics of geopolymer and fly ash particles', 'chunk_index': 5, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
