2025-07-17 21:21:50 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:182 - 创建分块 17: 数据 = {'id': '91cd1837-6493-46e4-8563-0226f033d3d8', 'document_id': '00dee3de-5908-4d9d-a0e2-dbbce470ae86', 'content': 'natural rubber (NR) matrix, for the NR/GP (a) and NR/FA (b) composites ![01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_8_626_133_1014_781_0.jpg](images/01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_8_626_133_1014_781_0.jpg) FIGURE 9 Stress-strain curves of gum, fly ash- and geopolymer (GP)-filled natural rubber vulcanizates with various geopolymer loadings or 10 phr fly ash. (The insert provides magnified view of below 100% extensions.) ### 3.5 | Mechanical properties Figure 9 shows the stress-strain curves for the gum, and the FA- and GP-filled NR vulcanizates with various geo-polymer loadings or with ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}$ fly ash. It is seen that the NR composites with 10 or ${20}\\mathrm{{phr}}\\mathrm{{GP}}$ exhibited higher Young’s and ${100}\\%$ moduli (i.e., final positions in the graph insert) and stress-strain curves raised above that of the gum vulcanizate. However, incorporating more than 20 phr GP (or 10 phr FA) gave similar stress-strain curves to the gum vulcanizate but with different failure location or elongation at break. The geopolymer at 10 or ${20}\\mathrm{{phr}}$ reduced the mobility of molecular chains and hence increased stiffness of the material. In contrast, incorporating 30 or 40 phr GP (or 10 phr FA) lowered the ${100}\\%$ modulus and the stress-strain curve from those of the gum NR vulcanizate. This might be due to agglomeration of filler (Figures 6 and 7) which reduced effective specific surface and reinforcing performance. This result agrees well with the high delta torque for the NR composite with ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}\\mathrm{{GP}}$ (Table 6).\nThe mechanical properties tensile strength and elongation at break, obtained from the failure points of the stress-strain curves in Figure 9, are plotted in Figure 10. It can be seen that the GP/NR (10 phr) vulcanizate exhibited higher tensile strength but lower elongation at break than the gum NR vulcanizate. This may be due to the interactions of geopolymer and NR that improved the strength but reduced the ability to extend. Furthermore, further increase in geopolymer loading decreased the tensile strength and the elongation at break. This again might be due to excessive filler loading creating flaws that initiate failure: voids, cavities, and filler aggregates (Figures 6 and 7). The lack of adhesion between rubber matrix and filler allowed formation of weak spots that degraded tensile strength and elongation at break. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {31}\\right\\rbrack }$', 'chunk_index': 17, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
